#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
MCP Julia REPL Adapter (multiplexing router)

This adapter is the MCP server as far as the client (Claude Code, Gemini, ...) is
concerned. It is launched once per project session over stdio, with its working
directory set to the project the agent is working in.

Multiple Julia REPLs can run at once, each on its own port, each advertising
itself with a `<pid>.json` file in ~/.mcprepl/registry/. This adapter discovers
those REPLs and routes every `tools/call` — but it keeps NO sticky selection.
Routing is decided fresh from the live registry on each call:

  * explicit `repl=<word>` (or MCPREPL_PROJECT)  -> route there, or ERROR if that
    word is not a live REPL (never silently fall back to a different one)
  * no target, exactly one shared REPL           -> use it
  * no target, >=2 shared REPLs                   -> return a listing so the
    caller retries with `repl=<word>` (we never silently guess among several)
  * no target, no shared REPL                     -> ERROR

The agent carries the chosen word via `repl=` and keeps passing it; that keeps
routing legible (visible in the agent's own transcript) instead of hidden in
adapter state. Proximity to our cwd is only ever a *suggestion* in the listing,
never an automatic pick among several. Julia stays a plain HTTP request/response
endpoint; all routing lives here.

The Julia REPL server(s) must be running first:
    julia --project -e "using MCPRepl; MCPRepl.start!()"
"""

